mm. This is getting complicated. Maybe the best approach is to discard a tile that's not part of any possible meld and is less valuable. \n\nConsidering all options, I think Play W5 is a good choice because it's a middle tile in the W suit, and I don't have other Ws to form a chow. Discarding it doesn't hinder my potential melds as much as discarding other tiles. \n\nSo the final answer is Play W5.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for forming chows in the T suit and W3 as a pair. Discarding W5, which is a middle tile in the W suit with no supporting tiles, minimizes disruption to possible melds.]\nReason: [W5 is a middle tile in the W suit with no adjacent tiles to form a chow. Discarding it preserves the potential for T1-T2-T3 and T4-T5-T6 chows. Keeping W3 as a pair supports a possible pung later.]\nAnswer: Play W5"}
